---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Battery Testing Methods';
const description = 'Battery testing methods — capacity, CCA, load, resistance, cycle and safety testing: what each measures, how it is measured, and the applicable standards.';
const canonical = '/testing/';

const collectionJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'CollectionPage',
  name: title,
  url: 'https://worldbatteryhub.com/testing/',
  about: 'Battery testing methods and standards',
};

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'How is CCA tested?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'The battery is cooled to −18°C (0°F), then discharged at the cold-cranking amperage for 30 seconds. It must hold at or above a cutoff voltage — 7.2V for a 12V battery under SAE J537 — for the rating to stand. EN 50342 and JIS D5301 use different temperatures and cutoffs, which is why CCA numbers differ between standards.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is a C20 capacity test?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'C20 is the 20-hour discharge rate used to rate a battery\u2019s capacity. The battery is discharged at a constant current over 20 hours until it reaches a cutoff voltage, and the amp-hours delivered is its C20 capacity. A faster rate (C10, C5) yields a lower capacity, which is why the rate must always be stated.',
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is the difference between internal resistance and conductance testing?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'Internal resistance (measured in milliohms) rises as a battery ages or sulfates; a higher value means a weaker battery. Conductance (measured in siemens) is its inverse and is what most handheld field testers read. Both are proxies for cranking ability, but they are not interchangeable with a true CCA load test.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  ogType="article"
  jsonLd={[collectionJsonLd, faqJsonLd]}
  author="World Battery Hub"
  dateModified="2026-09-17"
>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="byline">Testing · Last reviewed 2026-09-17</p>
      <h1>Battery Testing Methods</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Testing is where a battery&rsquo;s claims become measurements. Capacity, CCA, load, resistance, cycle life
        and safety are not opinions — they are numbers produced by defined procedures under defined conditions,
        and the test standard is what makes the number checkable.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>At a glance:</strong> Battery testing splits into <strong>performance</strong> (capacity, CCA,
        load, reserve), <strong>electrical condition</strong> (internal resistance, conductance, impedance),
        <strong>life</strong> (cycle, degradation, self-discharge) and <strong>safety</strong> (abuse, thermal,
        short-circuit, vibration). Every method resolves to a standard, a test condition and a pass criterion —
        and the condition is half the meaning of the number.
      </div>

      <h2>What this section covers</h2>
      <p>
        This hub is the entry point to the site&rsquo;s testing knowledge. It defines what each method measures,
        how the measurement is made, and which standard governs it. The detailed, standards-referenced method
        pages live on the
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/">testing sub-site</a>; the standards themselves are
        catalogued in the <a href="/standards/">standards directory</a>. The two work as one: a method says
        <em>what you do</em>, the standard says <em>under what conditions and to what pass mark</em>.
      </p>

      <h2>The testing method catalog</h2>
      <p>
        Twenty-one core methods, grouped by what they measure. Each links to its standards-referenced page on the
        testing sub-site.
      </p>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Measures</th><th>Main standard(s)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/cca-testing/">CCA testing</a></td><td>Cold-cranking ability</td><td>SAE J537, EN 50342, JIS D5301, DIN 43539</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/capacity-testing/">Capacity testing (C20)</a></td><td>Stored amp-hours at the 20-hour rate</td><td>IEC 60095, EN 50342</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/capacity-rates/">Capacity rates (C20/C10/C5)</a></td><td>How capacity falls at faster discharge rates</td><td>IEC 60095, EN 50342</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/reserve-capacity/">Reserve capacity (RC)</a></td><td>Minutes of 25 A discharge to 10.5V</td><td>BCI / SAE</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/load-testing/">Load testing</a></td><td>Voltage under a calibrated load</td><td>Manufacturer / field practice</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/charge-acceptance/">Charge acceptance</a></td><td>How readily the battery takes charge</td><td>EN 50342</td></tr>
