          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/vibration-testing/">Vibration testing</a></td><td>Mechanical integrity under vibration</td><td>UN 38.3, IEC 62660</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/leak-testing/">Leak testing</a></td><td>Electrolyte containment</td><td>IEC 60095, manufacturer method</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/bms-testing/">BMS testing</a></td><td>Protection and balancing functions</td><td>IEC 62619, UL 1973</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/acceptance-testing/">Acceptance testing (incoming QC)</a></td><td>Verification on receipt before installation</td><td>IEEE 1188, manufacturer spec</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        The testing sub-site also carries pressure, insulation-resistance and related specialised pages — see the
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/">full method index</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Standards that govern battery testing</h2>
      <p>
        Every method resolves to a standard. The four that anchor lead-acid testing are
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/sae-j537/">SAE J537</a> (North American cranking),
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/en-50342/">EN 50342</a> (European starter),
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/jis-d5301/">JIS D5301</a> (Japanese automotive) and
        <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/din-43539/">DIN 43539</a> (German cranking). For
        lithium, the anchors are <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/iec-62660/">IEC 62660</a>
        (EV cells), <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/iec-62133/">IEC 62133</a> (portable
        safety) and <a href="https://testing.worldbatteryhub.com/standards/un-38-3/">UN 38.3</a> (transport). The
        full directory is in the <a href="/standards/">standards hub</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Engineering interpretation</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>What the data means:</strong> A battery number without its test condition is marketing. The same
          battery can show different CCA under SAE, EN and DIN — not because it changed, but because the test
          temperature and cutoff changed. The useful habit is to always read a rating <em>with</em> its standard,
          and to treat cross-standard comparisons as translation, not arithmetic.</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Why it matters:</strong> Testing is the site&rsquo;s professional moat: it turns &ldquo;this
          battery is good&rdquo; into &ldquo;this battery delivers X under condition Y per standard Z&rdquo; — the
          difference between a blog and a knowledge platform that engineers and AI systems can both verify.</p>
        <p class="muted"><em>Engineering interpretation, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>How is CCA tested?</h3>
      <p>The battery is cooled to −18°C (0°F), then discharged at the cold-cranking amperage for 30 seconds. It must hold at or above a cutoff voltage — 7.2V for a 12V battery under SAE J537 — for the rating to stand. EN 50342 and JIS D5301 use different temperatures and cutoffs, which is why CCA numbers differ between standards.</p>
      <h3>What is a C20 capacity test?</h3>
      <p>C20 is the 20-hour discharge rate used to rate a battery&rsquo;s capacity. The battery is discharged at a constant current over 20 hours until it reaches a cutoff voltage, and the amp-hours delivered is its C20 capacity. A faster rate (C10, C5) yields a lower capacity, which is why the rate must always be stated.</p>
      <h3>What is the difference between internal resistance and conductance testing?</h3>
      <p>Internal resistance (measured in milliohms) rises as a battery ages or sulfates; a higher value means a weaker battery. Conductance (measured in siemens) is its inverse and is what most handheld field testers read. Both are proxies for cranking ability, but they are not interchangeable with a true CCA load test.</p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <p>Method definitions and standard conditions per the publishing organizations (SAE, EN/DIN, JIS, IEC, UL, UN) as cited on the linked testing sub-site pages.</p>

